 "circuits": [],
 "equations": [],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Polarity",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Lead 1 is the anode and lead 2 the cathode. The anode lead is at least 1.0 mm longer than the cathode lead."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Drive",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Run the lamp forward biased only. The drive circuit should apply neither forward nor reverse voltage while it is switched off; a reverse voltage held on the lamp for a long time can cause migration that damages it."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thermal derating",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Continuous forward current may stay at 25 mA up to about 27 °C ambient and then falls linearly to about 7 mA at 85 °C. Plan heat and current from this derating line and keep the air round the lamp under control."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Lead forming",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "Bend leads no closer than 3 mm to the base of the epoxy bulb, and form them before soldering, not after. Keep stress off the package while bending, since force at the base can change its characteristics or break it. Cut leads at room temperature. Board holes must line up with the leads so the lamp is not mounted under strain, which degrades the epoxy."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Storage",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "After shipment keep at 30 °C or below and 70 %RH or below; storage life under those conditions is 3 months. Beyond 3 months, a sealed container with nitrogen and desiccant extends it to one year. Avoid sudden temperature changes in humid air, where condensation forms."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Soldering",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "Keep the solder joint more than 3 mm from the epoxy bulb, ideally with the joint further out than where the tie bar ends. Hand or dip solder once only, at the lowest workable temperature, and put no stress on the leads while hot. Wave soldering is to be held at the recommended temperature and dwell time. After soldering, protect the bulb from shock and vibration until it is back at room temperature, and do not force-cool it from the peak."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Cleaning",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "If cleaning is needed, use isopropyl alcohol at room temperature for one minute at most, then let it dry at room temperature. Ultrasonic cleaning is not to be used unless qualified first for the power and assembly involved."
  },
  {
   "topic": "ESD",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "The die can be damaged by static or surge voltage. Handle with grounded wrist straps, footwear, clothing, floors, benches and tools, use conductive mats, and ground all assembly equipment. Where tools contain insulating parts such as glass or plastic, use conductive materials, humidity or ionizers to remove or prevent charge. Products built with it should include surge protection."
  }
 ],
